    "overview": "The green toad (Bufotes viridis) is a harmless, green-spotted toad of open country in Georgia. The 2026 Georgian checklist treats it as a biologically confirmed species and keeps the name Bufotes viridis. It belongs to the true toad family (Bufonidae). It has no venomous bite and is not dangerous to people. Species-level length is usually about 5–12 cm; females are generally larger than males. IUCN assesses it as Least Concern. It is not on Georgia’s 2014 Red List.",
    "habitat": "A 1996 Georgian review maps it across the Caucasian Isthmus — from forest to desert and into settled land — but it avoids closed-canopy forest, though it may appear in clearings. That paper lists 28 localities in eastern Georgia and 14 in the west; it is abundant across the country except on the humid south-western Black Sea coast. Records from Batumi and Poti are older (Nikolsky 1913 and later citations) and it is scarce there. Distribution confidence in the checklist is medium. The 2026 barcode and checklist figure are from Pantishara Canyon (Dedoplistskaro Municipality, Kakheti). Breeding in Borjomi Canyon is confirmed by 1993–1994 field studies; that site lies at about 900–1200 m — a local study range, not a country-wide elevation limit. This atlas marks all 12 regions, including Kakheti (Pantishara) and Samtskhe–Javakheti (Borjomi); western coastal regions are included because of those older records and should be treated as scarce. Look for it in steppe, clearings, gardens, parks and other open settled land. It breeds in shallow pools, ditches and ponds — at species level usually not deeper than 50 cm.",
