    "behavior": "It is mainly active at dusk and at night. By day it stays hidden. During breeding it may also appear by day around shallow water. In dry weather it may come to water at night to drink. In Borjomi Canyon the peak of breeding has been recorded in April–June. It is an open-country toad: in Borjomi females may lay more than one clutch in a season, unlike the forest toad, which usually lays one. Eggs are laid in long strings in water. Females there typically mature after three winters; the oldest females in that sample reached 9–12 years. It overwinters on land — in burrows, holes and under stones; sometimes in water. If you find one, leave it where it is and do not pick it up. Wash your hands after any contact. It is not dangerous to people.",
