      {
        "question": "How large does it grow?",
        "answer": "Adults are usually about 7–11 cm in total length; breeding males are described up to about 12 cm. Separate Georgian measurements are not given on this page."
      },
      {
        "question": "What does it eat?",
        "answer": "In water — small aquatic invertebrates. On land — soil invertebrates. There is no Georgian diet study on this page."
      },
      {
        "question": "When is it active?",
        "answer": "It is in water when breeding, mostly in spring; at other times it is often on land. A full calendar for every Georgian population is not given here. At higher elevation the season is later."
      },
      {
        "question": "Why is it a candidate species?",
        "answer": "It is an isolated lineage in the L. vulgaris complex. Some authors treat it as a species, others as a subspecies or a synonym. The checklist keeps it as a candidate until rank is tested further."
      },
      {
        "question": "What should I do if I find one?",
        "answer": "Watch it and leave it where it is. Do not pick it up or move it to another pond. It is not a medical risk to people."
      }
